//- The first page of the manual. Params: d.
div(data-ui="prose")
  raw p("**Fablecut is a studio for making short videos with code.** Every picture you see in a Fablecut video is drawn by a small JavaScript function, called an **asset**, that knows how to draw one frame at any moment in time. You arrange assets on a timeline to make a **clip**, and the studio renders the clip, frame by frame, into an MP4 file you can publish.")
  p The twist is that nothing you make is thrown away. Every asset you write or tune stays in a searchable library, with every version kept, so the next video starts from everything the last one left behind. That is why the studio calls itself a library that compounds: each clip is cheaper to make than the one before it.
  raw p("You can work in two ways, and mix them freely: **by hand**, in the studio's screens in your browser, and **by asking Claude**, which drives the same studio through its MCP tools and can answer requests you type into the studio.")

  h2#right-now The studio right now
  p These numbers come from the studio that drew this page, so they are always current.
  div(data-ui="grid" data-cols="1" data-cols-sm="3" data-gap="4")
    +stat(variant="card")
      @value
        | {{ d.live.counts.assets }}
      @label
        | assets in the library
    +stat(variant="card")
      @value
        | {{ d.live.counts.clips }}
      @label
        | clips
    +stat(variant="card")
      @value
        | {{ d.live.counts.renders }}
      @label
        | renders made
  raw p("Your work is kept in the data folder `" + d.live.dataDir + "`. See [Settings and folders](/docs/config) for what is in it.")

  h2#where-to-start Where to start
  div(data-ui="grid" data-cols="1" data-cols-sm="2" data-gap="4")
    +card(title="New here?")
      @body
        raw p("Read [The big ideas](/docs/concepts) (five minutes), then follow [Your first clip](/docs/first-clip). You will have a rendered video at the end.")
    +card(title="Setting it up")
      @body
        raw p("[Install and run](/docs/install) lists what you need (Node 24 and FFmpeg) and the three commands that start the studio.")
    +card(title="Using the screens")
      @body
        raw p("Start with [The library](/docs/library) and [The clip editor](/docs/editor). [Keyboard and mouse](/docs/shortcuts) has every shortcut on one page.")
    +card(title="Working with Claude")
      @body
        raw p("[Claude Code and the studio](/docs/claude) shows what to ask for, and the [MCP tool reference](/docs/mcp-tools) lists every tool Claude can call.")

  h2#what-it-is-good-at What Fablecut is good at
  ul
    li Motion graphics: kinetic titles, lower thirds, charts that draw themselves, logo stings, end cards.
    li Explainers and product tours: screen recordings in a browser frame, with a cursor, a spotlight and callouts.
    li Voiced videos with captions: a narration with the time of every word, captions built from those words, and music that ducks under the voice.
    li The same video in several shapes: one composition laid out for vertical (9:16), horizontal (16:9) and square (1:1).
    li Videos that must be right: the studio checks text size, contrast, safe zones, captions, loudness and licences before you publish.
    li
      raw md("Clips shared as a link: a page that plays the clip in any phone or desktop browser, with no video site in between. See [Share a clip as a link](/docs/share).")
    li
      raw md("Clips that are played, not watched: slides, a quiz, a menu, a small game. See [Interactive clips](/docs/interactive).")
  raw p("It is not a camera-footage editor: it can place and trim video files ([Pictures, video and fonts](/docs/media)), but its strength is everything drawn by code. A clip is at most " + d.live.maxClipSeconds + " seconds long.")

  h2#how-this-manual-works How this manual is organised
  dl
    dt Start here
    dd What Fablecut is, how to install it, the ideas behind it, and a guided first clip.
    dt The studio
    dd One page per screen of the studio, plus every shortcut and the request flow for asking the agent.
    dt Making videos
    dd How a clip is built, how to write assets, media, sound and captions, the checks, licences and the repository.
    dt Share and play
    dd A clip as a link that plays in the viewer's browser, and interactive clips: scenes, variables, wires and their checks.
    dt Working with Claude
    dd How Claude Code drives the studio, and the reference of every MCP tool.
    dt Reference
    dd Settings, folders, commands, troubleshooting and a glossary.
  +callout(variant="info")
    @content
      raw p("**Looking for something specific?** Use the search box at the top of every page. It searches every section of the manual and takes you straight to the one that matches.")
